import "core"


# A data type eighteen fields wide, declared, shown, and printed, which is what holds that a type
  costs what its width says it costs.
  Typechecking does not CONVERGE any slower for the width. This file is 9 epochs over 3 fragments,
  and so is the same shape at twenty-four fields, at thirty-two and at forty-eight. Measured with
  @catln epochs@ against @io.ct@, which declares nothing, the whole compilation is 799 epochs where
  that baseline is 793: six epochs for a type eighteen fields wide. A page is partitioned into solve
  fragments per function SCC, so a declaration this wide is its own small component rather than
  something the rest of the page is solved against.
  A width in the STANDARD LIBRARY costs nothing per PAGE either: widening
  [/Data/Note/Task/Obsidian] from ten fields to seventeen leaves @catln epochs io.ct@ at 56s and
  @catln tbuild io.ct@ at 58s, both within a second of what they are without it. It is not free
  across a whole PROGRAM, though — that same widening takes the integration suite from twenty-two
  minutes to over an hour and 6.5GB, which is the reason the comment on that type gives for the
  fields it still carries as JSON. So measure a width against the thing being compiled: a page at a
  time says one thing here and a suite of them says another.
  Building the arrow trees for it is where a width DOES still cost, and only for a value something
  READS. Against @catln tbuild io.ct@, which declares nothing and is 58s:
    18 fields, printed     —  60s
    24 fields, printed     — 252s
    24 fields, NOT printed —  60s
  So the same twenty-four-field declaration is free until something reads it, and eighteen is below
  where that climb starts. That is why the value below is read back and not only declared: without
  a reading of it the program typechecks, builds no tree for the value, and this file would pass
  while saying nothing about the half of the cost that is real.
  Eighteen and not a round number, because that is what a checkbox is. A note in a vault running the
  Obsidian Tasks plugin carries a status, a description, tags, a priority, six dates, a recurrence,
  an action on completion, an id, the ids it waits on and a block link — eighteen things, which is
  the width below. [/Data/Note/Task/Obsidian] is the type such a checkbox is read into.
data(Wide(a -> String, b -> String, c -> String, d -> String, e -> String, f -> String, g -> String, h -> String, i -> String, j -> String, k -> String, l -> String, m -> String, n -> String, o -> String, p -> String, q -> String, r -> String))

every(Wide.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

/Wide(a= "1", b= "2", c= "3", d= "4", e= "5", f= "6", g= "7", h= "8", i= "9", j= "10", k= "11", l= "12", m= "13", n= "14", o= "15", p= "16", q= "17", r= "18")

# Read back so that the value is built and not only declared, as @valueDeclFields.ct@ does and for
  the same reason: the width that costs anything is the width something reads. The first and the
  last field, so the reading reaches across the whole width.
firstOf(w -> Wide..) -> String
firstOf(w= Wide(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r))= a

lastOf(w -> Wide..) -> String
lastOf(w= Wide(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r))= r

main{io -> IO} -> (){IO} =
  #assert(test= firstOf(w= /Wide(a= "1", b= "2", c= "3", d= "4", e= "5", f= "6", g= "7", h= "8", i= "9", j= "10", k= "11", l= "12", m= "13", n= "14", o= "15", p= "16", q= "17", r= "18")) == "1")
  #assert(test= lastOf(w= /Wide(a= "1", b= "2", c= "3", d= "4", e= "5", f= "6", g= "7", h= "8", i= "9", j= "10", k= "11", l= "12", m= "13", n= "14", o= "15", p= "16", q= "17", r= "18")) == "18")
  ()
